1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,380
Meet Voice Studio. A small Mac command line tool with a browser

2
00:00:05,380 --> 00:00:10,759
interface. It turns text into downloadable MP3 narration using installed Siri voices.

3
00:00:10,759 --> 00:00:16,139
This video's narration was made with Voice Studio itself, using Nora.

4
00:00:16,839 --> 00:00:21,107
Start with the one line installer at the top of the GitHub

5
00:00:21,107 --> 00:00:25,376
readme. You need macOS, Apple command line tools, and a downloaded natural

6
00:00:25,376 --> 00:00:29,645
Siri voice. The installer uses your existing encoder, or installs ffmpeg through

7
00:00:29,645 --> 00:00:33,914
an existing Homebrew setup. Then run the installed voice studio command. Leave

8
00:00:33,914 --> 00:00:38,183
Terminal open while you work.

9
00:00:38,883 --> 00:00:43,360
In Create audio, paste your script. Choose the language and region, then

10
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:47,837
an installed Siri voice. Nora is selected when available. Click Generate MP3.

11
00:00:47,837 --> 00:00:52,314
The audio plays when it is ready, and Download MP3 saves a

12
00:00:52,314 --> 00:00:56,791
copy. If your browser blocks autoplay, press Play.

13
00:00:57,491 --> 00:01:01,932
Move the speech speed slider left for slower speech, or right for

14
00:01:01,932 --> 00:01:06,374
faster speech. It changes the generated recording, not the player's playback speed.

15
00:01:06,374 --> 00:01:10,815
After the first successful render, changing language, voice, or speed regenerates and

16
00:01:10,815 --> 00:01:15,257
plays updated audio. Rapid changes are combined. Editing text alone still needs

17
00:01:15,257 --> 00:01:19,699
Generate MP3.

18
00:01:20,399 --> 00:01:24,358
Agents with shell access on the same Mac can use the included

19
00:01:24,358 --> 00:01:28,317
Python helper. Ask Codex or Claude to read a script file, use

20
00:01:28,317 --> 00:01:32,277
Nora, and save a voiceover MP3. The helper starts Voice Studio without

21
00:01:32,277 --> 00:01:36,236
a browser, checks readiness, generates the recording, and shuts down its own

22
00:01:36,236 --> 00:01:40,196
server. Python three is required for this helper.

23
00:01:40,896 --> 00:01:45,109
For a custom agent workflow, start Voice Studio with no open. Read

24
00:01:45,109 --> 00:01:49,323
the private session URL from Terminal. Send the token as a bearer

25
00:01:49,323 --> 00:01:53,536
authorization header. Check status, post text, a voice identifier, and rate to

26
00:01:53,536 --> 00:01:57,749
generate, then download the returned audio path. Run requests one at a

27
00:01:57,749 --> 00:02:01,963
time and keep the token private.

28
00:02:02,663 --> 00:02:06,652
Use ffprobe to verify that the saved file is MP3 with a

29
00:02:06,652 --> 00:02:10,641
nonzero duration. Your agent can then use it in a demo, presentation,

30
00:02:10,641 --> 00:02:14,631
or video. The server is local to this Mac. Download recordings before

31
00:02:14,631 --> 00:02:18,620
stopping the CLI, because Control C deletes temporary session audio. Saved files

32
00:02:18,620 --> 00:02:22,609
remain yours.

33
00:02:23,309 --> 00:02:27,029
The GitHub project includes the installer, screenshots, a detailed usage guide, and

34
00:02:27,029 --> 00:02:30,749
copyable agent instructions. Start with a short script, choose Nora, and generate

35
00:02:30,749 --> 00:02:34,469
your first recording. The same local tool works for you and your

36
00:02:34,469 --> 00:02:38,189
coding agents.
